---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import GCounterDemo from "../../components/GCounterDemo.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Counters"
  description="Start with a grow-only counter. Run concurrent increments through Watershed Sluice and see why resending a component does not count twice."
  territory="Structures"
  sheetTitle="Counters"
>
  <article class="structure-page">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="territory-label">Structures</p>
      <h1>Counters</h1>
      <p class="structure-summary">
        A replicated counter must preserve increments made at different
        replicas. Start with the smallest useful form: a counter that can only
        grow.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="sluice-title">
      <h2 id="sluice-title">Sluice sequences and relays changes</h2>
      <p>
        Replicas in a distributed data structure need a way to exchange their
        changes. They can send changes directly to their peers, or they can
        send them through a server. The demos on this site use Sluice, a small
        server sequencer. It accepts operations from the clients, puts them in
        one total order, and relays them to every connected replica.
      </p>
      <p>
        Data structures define different operations and use the ordered stream
        in different ways. Some use the sequence number to resolve changes.
        Others, including the G-counter below, do not use it in their merge
        rule. They still need the relay so each replica receives the changes.
        A server sequencer provides one transport model that can carry all of
        these data structures.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sluice is not a data structure, and it does not decide how changes
        merge. It plays the server's role inside the demos and lets them pause
        delivery so clients can change independently. You can then release the
        queued operations and watch the replicas converge.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="value-title">
      <h2 id="value-title">The value is familiar</h2>
      <p>
        A G-counter stores one nonnegative count for each replica. A replica
        changes only its own count. The visible value is the sum of every
        component it has observed.
      </p>
      <pre aria-label="G-counter value rule"><code>value = count[A] + count[B] + ...</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="race-title">
      <h2 id="race-title">What if A and B increment before delivery?</h2>
      <p>
        Replica A adds 7 while replica B adds 3. Replica C makes no local
        change. All three start from the same zero state, and Sluice holds the
        two operations until you deliver them. Run that race, or use the
        controls on each client to make your own.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GCounterDemo />

    <section aria-labelledby="result-title">
      <h2 id="result-title">Both increments survive</h2>
      <p>
        After delivery, all three replicas read 10. A did not overwrite B, and
        B did not overwrite A. Each writer owns a separate component, so merge
        can keep both facts and C can learn the complete value.
      </p>
      <p>
        For each replica ID, merge keeps the larger count. This rule is
        commutative, associative, and idempotent. Arrival order does not change
        the         answer, and the same component can arrive more than once.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="limits-title">
      <h2 id="limits-title">The simple rule has a strict limit</h2>
      <p>
        Counts never decrease. A negative increment is rejected before state
        changes. Every writer also needs a unique replica ID; if two writers
        share one ID, pairwise maximum can discard one concurrent increment.
      </p>
      <table class="counter-family" aria-label="Counter family status">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Counter</th>
            <th scope="col">Use</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">G-counter</th>
            <td>Increment only</td>
            <td>Read now</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Operation counter</th>
            <td>Add delivered operations once</td>
            <td>Planned</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">PN-counter</th>
            <td>Increment and decrement</td>
            <td>Planned</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="notes-title">
      <h2 id="notes-title">Field notes</h2>
      <p>
        This lesson calls Watershed's public{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/src/watershed/g_counter_kernel.gleam">G-counter kernel</a>.
        The three clients run through Watershed's{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/src/watershed/sluice_js.gleam">JavaScript Sluice transport</a>.
        Its interaction follows Watershed's focused{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/website/src/components/Demo.astro">counter demo</a>
        {" "}and{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/website/src/scripts/demo.ts">runtime</a>,
        while Atlas owns the three-client lesson schedule and presentation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The merge rule is the state-based grow-only counter described in{" "}
        <a href="https://inria.hal.science/inria-00555588/document">A comprehensive study of Convergent and Commutative Replicated Data Types</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="structure-next" aria-label="Continue through structures">
      <a href="/structures/">Browse all structures</a>
      <a href="/atlas/multi-value-registers/">Continue to registers</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .structure-page {
    max-width: var(--measure-reading);
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .structure-page > section {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
  }

  .structure-summary {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  pre {
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border-block: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
    background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--clear) 45%, var(--sky-sheet));
  }

  .counter-family {
    width: 100%;
    margin-block: var(--space-5);
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }

  .counter-family :is(th, td) {
    padding: var(--space-3);
    border-block: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .counter-family th {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
  }

  .counter-family td:last-child {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  .structure-next {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
    padding-block: var(--space-5);
    border-top: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-weight: 650;
  }
</style>
